#!RuleTest
#test CDISC-SEND-0249 expect=violation domain=DM
#note "Authority: SEND Conformance Rules v5.0 SEND249 (SENDIG 3.0 only), Rule 'STUDYID = TS.STUDYID'; Plain Text: 'The STUDYID in all domains must match the STUDYID in TS.' The primary is DM, not TS: the rule is scope ALL and with TS as the primary a foreign-bound and a primary-bound read of STUDYID would agree. STUDYID is Required, so no row leaves it blank (the Check's not-empty guard is not exercised). Row 2 carries STUDYID=S2 while TS holds only S1 -> FIRES. Row 1 matches TS -> silent. ALSO THE S3 FOREIGN-DISCRIMINATION PIN (SKELETON-distinct-function hazard 3): S2 exists only in the primary DM, so a port that read STUDYID from DM instead of TS would find row 2's own value in its own set and fall silent."
#library standard=sendig version=3-0
#expectViolationCount 1
#expectViolationAt row=2 USUBJID=S2-002 STUDYID=S2
dataset DM class="SPECIAL PURPOSE" label="Demographics"
col STUDYID type=Char label="Study Identifier"
col DOMAIN  type=Char label="Domain Abbreviation"
col USUBJID type=Char label="Unique Subject Identifier"
col SUBJID  type=Char label="Subject Identifier for the Study"
col SEX     type=Char label="Sex"
---
S1 | DM | S1-001 | 001 | M
S2 | DM | S2-002 | 002 | F
---

dataset TS label="Trial Summary"
col STUDYID  type=Char label="Study Identifier"
col DOMAIN   type=Char label="Domain Abbreviation"
col TSSEQ    type=Num  label="Sequence Number"
col TSPARMCD type=Char label="Trial Summary Parameter Short Name"
col TSPARM   type=Char label="Trial Summary Parameter"
col TSVAL    type=Char label="Parameter Value"
---
S1 | TS | 1 | SPECIES | Species          | RAT
S1 | TS | 2 | STRAIN  | Strain/Substrain | SPRAGUE-DAWLEY
---
